<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The PlayStation backend for the portable presentation layer: implementations of Ripcord.Presentation's
    discovery, registration and reachability seams in terms of Halyard, plus the composition root that wires
    them together. The one place a front end has to name Halyard at all.

    This is the crypto-seam pattern from CLAUDE.md applied to the app layer rather than to crypto. Keeping the
    Halyard dependency here instead of in Ripcord.Presentation buys three concrete things:

      1. Ripcord.Presentation.Tests references exactly one project, so the pairing state machine — the most
         valuable thing in this layer to test — is tested with no UDP, no ports 9302/987, no registration
         cipher and no dirty-room fixtures.
      2. ConsoleFamily exists to be the boundary between the vendor-codename vocabulary and the product-name
         vocabulary. If Ripcord.Presentation referenced Halyard, HalyardConsolePlatform would leak back across
         that boundary by convenience and the boundary would stop meaning anything.
      3. A second console family gets added by implementing the seams here, not by editing the shared layer.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ripcord.Presentation\Ripcord.Presentation.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ripcord.Protocol.Halyard\Ripcord.Protocol.Halyard.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Ripcord.Protocol.Halyard.Common\Ripcord.Protocol.Halyard.Common.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
